  // Agencies Page
  "agencies.hero.title": "The client keeps the database. You keep the code.",
  "agencies.hero.subtitle": "You build the backend and the back office on a Postgres the client owns, under a licence nobody can revoke. When the engagement ends, they keep the database and you keep the code — and neither of you is renting the thing in between.",
  "agencies.weapon.title": "The backend is the deliverable. The panel is the part they see.",
  "agencies.weapon.desc1": "Every client project needs the same layer: REST over their tables, a typed client, sessions, file uploads, permissions that hold. It is the least differentiated work on the invoice and the easiest to get subtly wrong.",
  "agencies.weapon.desc2": "Rebase generates it from the client's own schema, with the access rules compiled into Postgres row-level security rather than into middleware your successor has to remember. The panel comes off the same definition, so the operator team gets a back office without a second sprint for it.",
  "agencies.accelerate.title": "What you stop writing per client",
  "agencies.accelerate.f1.title": "The CRUD layer",
  "agencies.accelerate.f1.desc": "Controllers, serializers, validation and the OpenAPI document, generated from the collection definition and regenerated when it changes.",
  "agencies.accelerate.f2.title": "The permission checks",
  "agencies.accelerate.f2.desc": "Written once beside the collection, compiled into Postgres policies. A table with no rule is refused rather than served, so a forgotten endpoint cannot leak it.",
  "agencies.accelerate.f3.title": "The back office",
  "agencies.accelerate.f3.desc": "Tables, forms, media, relations and roles render from the same definition, and your own React components go in wherever the generated field is not enough.",
  "agencies.customize.title": "Where the generated stops, your React starts",
  "agencies.customize.desc1": "A custom field, a custom preview, a whole custom view or an entity action — each is a React component referenced from the collection, not a fork of the panel.",
  "agencies.customize.desc2": "Branding, theme and copy are configuration, so the same codebase can be handed to two clients that look nothing alike.",
  "agencies.features.title": "What carries between projects",
  "agencies.features.subtitle": "Written once for one client, reused on the next without asking anyone's permission.",
  "agencies.features.f1.title": "Reusable components",
  "agencies.features.f1.desc": "Build custom fields and views once, reuse across client projects.",
  "agencies.features.f2.title": "White-label by configuration",
  "agencies.features.f2.desc": "Logo, palette and copy are config values, not a patched build.",
  "agencies.features.f3.title": "Granular permissions",
  "agencies.features.f3.desc": "Per collection, per field, per role — and enforced by Postgres, not by the panel.",
  "agencies.features.f4.title": "Deploys where the client already is",
  "agencies.features.f4.desc": "A published container and a Postgres connection string. Docker, Fly, Railway, Hetzner or their own metal.",
  "agencies.features.f5.title": "Cloud or self-hosted",
  "agencies.features.f5.desc": "Self-host on the client's own infrastructure, or run it on Rebase Cloud — in private beta today.",
  "agencies.features.f6.title": "React and TypeScript, all the way down",
  "agencies.features.f6.desc": "No template language, no proprietary builder. The people you already employ can read it.",
  "about.meta.title": "About — Rebase",
  "about.meta.description": "Why we build Rebase: authorization belongs in the database, not in middleware you can forget to call. Built by the team behind FireCMS, MIT-licensed, in Europe.",
  "about.hero.title": "Authorization belongs in the database.",
  "about.hero.subtitle": "That is the bet the whole product is built on, and the reason this company exists. Access rules written in application code protect the door, not the room — a forgotten endpoint, a background job, a migration script or a psql session walks straight past them. So we put them where the data is.",
  "about.story.title": "Why we build it",
  "about.story.p1": "Every backend we shipped before this one eventually leaked. Not through a dramatic breach — through the ordinary path: a new endpoint that forgot the permission check, an admin script run against production, an internal tool that used the service key because it was quicker. The rules were correct and the rules were bypassable, which is the same as not having them.",
  "about.story.p2": "Postgres already had the answer. Row-level security is enforced by the database on every statement and every caller, and it does not care which service forgot to ask. So Rebase compiles your collection definition into real policies, and refuses to serve a table that has none. That decision is what everything else on this site follows from: the API, the typed SDK, the panel — all of them are clients under the same rules, and none of them is a back door.",
  "about.story.p3": "Rebase is the second time we have built this. The first was <b class=\"text-white font-medium\">FireCMS</b> — an open-source CMS for Firebase used in more than 10,000 projects, by teams at companies including <b class=\"text-white font-medium\">Microsoft</b> and <b class=\"text-white font-medium\">IKEA</b>. Rebase applies what those years taught us to a database you own outright: standard Postgres, running wherever you choose to put it.",
  "about.values.title": "What we hold to",
  "about.values.v1.title": "Fail closed, even when it is inconvenient",
  "about.values.v1.desc": "A table with no policy is refused, not leaked. Your first query may come back empty until you write a rule. We would rather cost you five minutes than ship you the other failure mode.",
  "about.values.v2.title": "Nothing is held back",
  "about.values.v2.desc": "MIT, end to end — the schema editor, the generated APIs, the typed SDK, the panel. No feature is fenced off behind a paid tier, because a security model you cannot read is not one you can trust.",
  "about.values.v3.title": "You keep the database",
  "about.values.v3.desc": "Rebase runs in front of a Postgres you own. It never ingests a second copy, and stopping means the data is already where you left it. Leaving is a redeploy, not a migration.",
  "about.stats.title": "What that adds up to",
  "about.stats.s1.value": "100%",
  "about.stats.s1.label": "Open source — MIT, no feature gates",
  "about.stats.s3.value": "1.3k+",
  "about.stats.s3.label": "GitHub stars across our projects",
  "about.join.title": "Where the project lives",
  "about.join.desc": "The code, the issues and the arguments are all in the open. Come and disagree with us.",
  "about.join.github": "Contribute on GitHub",
  "about.join.discord": "Join our Discord",
